honor-fmbp (1.0.5) resolute; urgency=medium

  * honor-fmbp-hwmon-dkms, honor-fmbp-kbdlight-dkms: ship a modules-load.d
    drop-in so the driver actually loads on a clean boot. Both modules have no
    device modalias (they instantiate the fan hwmon / huawei::kbd_backlight LED
    unconditionally on the FMB-P), so nothing was loading them at boot on a
    fresh install — the fan RPM sensors and the GNOME keyboard-backlight control
    were silently absent until a manual modprobe. Each DKMS package now installs
    /usr/lib/modules-load.d/<module>.conf and the build guard requires it.
    (Exposed by the 2026-07-11 clean reinstall.)

 -- Philip Walsh <packages@drphilth.com>  Sat, 11 Jul 2026 19:48:19 +0100

honor-fmbp (1.0.4) resolute; urgency=medium

  * honor-fmbp-dsdt: support dracut as well as initramfs-tools, and stop
    forcing a generator swap. Ubuntu 26.04 defaults to dracut, so the old
    "Depends: initramfs-tools" pulled initramfs-tools over the network and
    removed dracut on a fresh install — breaking the offline pre-first-boot
    guarantee. Now:
    - Depends: initramfs-tools | dracut (satisfied by whichever is already
      present; no network fetch, no swap).
    - ship /etc/dracut.conf.d/10-honor-fmbp-dsdt.conf (acpi_override +
      acpi_table_dir) alongside the existing initramfs-tools hook; only the
      active generator's mechanism fires.
    - honor-fmbp-dsdt-select now also keeps /usr/share/honor-fmbp/acpi/ (the
      dracut table dir) holding just the selected SKU's AML, and rebuilds via
      update-initramfs (generator-agnostic on Ubuntu; dracut ships the shim),
      falling back to `dracut --force --regenerate-all` if only dracut-core
      is present.

 -- Philip Walsh <packages@drphilth.com>  Tue, 07 Jul 2026 16:36:11 +0100

honor-fmbp (1.0.3) resolute; urgency=medium

  * New binary honor-fmbp-archive-keyring: ships the ppa:drphilth/honor-fmbp
    deb822 .sources file and the PPA's signing key under /usr/share/keyrings.
    Installing it "adds the PPA" purely by dropping files — no
    add-apt-repository, no keyserver round-trip — so it can be applied from the
    installer chroot with no network; the repo is only contacted later on apt
    update/upgrade. The metapackage now depends on it. Launchpad mints a PPA
    signing key only on first upload, so the key is a placeholder until
    keyring/fetch-ppa-key.sh is run post-publish; debian/rules excludes the
    keyring binary (rather than blocking the whole build) while the placeholder
    is present, keeping the boot-critical packages buildable for test ISOs.
  * honor-magicbook-pro-14: reword the description — the Intel SOF audio
    firmware is pulled from the Ubuntu archive (firmware-sof-signed), not
    shipped by this package.

 -- Philip Walsh <packages@drphilth.com>  Sat, 04 Jul 2026 11:00:00 +0100

honor-fmbp (1.0.2) resolute; urgency=medium

  * honor-fmbp-dsdt: support both FMB-P BIOS SKUs. Ship the corrected DSDT for
    the global and Chinese variants (they differ only in six NVS base
    addresses) and pick the right one at install by matching the live firmware
    DSDT's GNVS signature (new honor-fmbp-dsdt-select, run from postinst; the
    initramfs hook is now variant-agnostic). The Chinese AML is derived from
    the same fixes but is untested on real Chinese hardware — see
    README.Debian. Override with /etc/honor-fmbp/dsdt-force. Global path
    unchanged (byte-identical AML).
  * honor-fmbp-config: ship honor-battery-thresholds.service (previously hand-
    installed), configurable via /etc/default/honor-fmbp-battery; restores the
    EC charge-limit preset at boot (default 70-90%).
  * honor-fmbp-config: pre-seed the maniacx "Battery Health Charging" GNOME
    extension's presets (dconf system default) to the ranges huawei-wmi
    enforces, so its Quick Settings panel is correct out of the box.
  * honor-magicbook-pro-14: Recommends gnome-shell-extension-manager.

 -- Philip Walsh <packages@drphilth.com>  Fri, 03 Jul 2026 15:00:00 +0100

honor-fmbp (1.0.1) resolute; urgency=high

  * Restore the per-package payload wiring (.install/.dkms/.initramfs-hook/
    .postinst) accidentally deleted in commit 97bd4eb; the 1.0.0 debs rebuilt
    after that deletion were empty (docs only), and reinstalling them made
    dpkg remove the DSDT override and DKMS sources — unbootable system
    (no NVMe) on 2026-07-02.
  * debian/rules: fail the build unless every staged payload file exists, so
    an empty package can never be produced silently again.

 -- Philip Walsh <packages@drphilth.com>  Fri, 03 Jul 2026 12:00:00 +0100

